{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 30, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "the social network (2010) is a masterfully crafted film that dissects the birth of facebook and the unraveling of its creator, mark zuckerberg’s, personal life. directed by david fincher, the movie is a scathing portrayal of ambition, betrayal, and the cost of success, all wrapped in razor-sharp dialogue and a hypnotic score by trent reznor and atticus finch.\n\nthe story unfolds through a series of flashbacks and legal depositions, framing zuckerberg (played with icy precision by jesse eisenberg) as a socially inept prodigy whose creation of facebook—a platform that connects billions—becomes both his triumph and his undoing. the film isn’t just about the rise of a tech titan; it’s a character study of a man who sacrifices empathy for validation, and the friends-turned-foes (like sean parker, played by justin timberlake in a scene-stealing role) who exploit his vision.\n\nthe script, written by aaron sorkin, crackles with wit and venom. lines like *\"you’re going to go through life thinking girls don’t like you because you’re a nerd. but it’ll be because you’re an asshole\"* cut deep, blending humor with heartbreak. the supporting cast is stellar: andrew garfield as eduardo severin, the loyal friend betrayed, armie hammer as the winklevoss twins (a masterclass in physical comedy and dual roles), and max minghella as divya narendra, all add layers to the moral ambiguity of the story.\n\nfincher’s direction is sleek and controlled, using cold blues and stark whites to mirror the sterile, transactional nature of zuckerberg’s world. the editing (by kirk baxter and angus wall) is seamless, weaving through time with a precision that mirrors the fast-paced, cutthroat world of silicon valley.\n\nwhile the film takes some creative liberties (like the invention of the \"harvard connection\" and zuckerberg’s portrayal), it captures the essence of the era: the arrogance of youth, the speed of innovation, and the loneliness of being the smartest guy in the room. the social network isn’t just a tech drama—it’s a modern tragedy about the hollow victory of winning the game while losing your soul.\n\nfinal verdict: a razor-sharp, dialogue-driven masterpiece that’s as entertaining as it is thought-provoking. 10/10.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.3156192898750305}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -0.0038729910738766193}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": " (", "logprob": -1.0909993648529053}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.0004808938247151673}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -1.6689286894688848e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -2.264974000354414e-06}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": ")", "logprob": -0.38702094554901123}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.09109127521514893}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.012572435662150383}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -0.9692525863647461}, {"token": "fully", "logprob": -0.5161520838737488}, {"token": " crafted", "logprob": -0.024043647572398186}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.614141583442688}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.1699802130460739}, {"token": " disse", "logprob": -0.6340399384498596}, {"token": "ct", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": "s", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.3139484226703644}, {"token": " birth", "logprob": -0.32511183619499207}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.1609395444393158}, {"token": " facebook", "logprob": -0.02079494297504425}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.32161691784858704}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.008710130117833614}, {"token": " un", "logprob": -8.305357933044434}, {"token": "ravel", "logprob": -0.37435922026634216}, {"token": "ing", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.31557390093803406}, {"token": " its", "logprob": -0.10645601898431778}, {"token": " creator", "logprob": -0.26384416222572327}, 